const abstracts = [
    { "A_ID": 12,
      "title": "Judgments of agency and culpability for AI use in college" ,
      "authors": "Thomas Konnick, Steve Mansfield, and Cory Potts",
      "affilation": ["SUNY Plattsburgh, Plattsburgh, NY"],
      "abstract": "Student use of artificial intelligence (AI) in academic work is rapidly increasing, ranging from minor grammatical assistance to full generation of written content. Because these scenarios reflect varying degrees of human-AI contribution, they raise important questions about students’ sense of agency over AI-assisted work. Specifically, do students feel agency over content generated by AI? In the present study, participants read a series of short scenarios describing different ways a student used AI to complete an essay, ranging from no AI input to fully AI-generated work. Participants rated the extent to which the student possessed agency over the essay. Results indicated that perceived agency scaled with the level of student involvement, such that greater human contribution was associated with higher agency ratings. However, frequent AI users attributed greater agency to the student across the scenarios (<i>F</i><sub>2,68</sub>=6.89, <i>p</i>&lt,.001, <i>η</i>²=.17). Notably, a dissociation emerged between agency and culpability: participants indicated that the student is accountable for any errors in the essay, even when it was largely or entirely AI-generated. Taken together, these findings have implications for how responsibility and authorship are conceptualized in educational settings that increasingly involve AI tools."
    },
{
    "A_ID": 4,
	"title":"Appearance-Related Social Media Consciousness Across Cultures: Psychometric Evaluation and Measurement Invariance in Iranian and U.S. University Students",
	"authors":"Shahrzad Ahmadkarajiᵃ, Koosha Orfiᵇ, Maryam Habibi Khormoojiᶜ",
	"affilation": ["ᵃUniversity of Vermont, Burlington, VT","ᵇDepartment of Communication, University of Delaware, Delaware, USA","ᶜSchool of Behavioral Sciences and Mental Health, Tehran Institute of Psychiatry, Iran University of Medical Sciences, Tehran, Iran"],
	"abstract": "Appearance-related social media experiences are increasingly implicated in body image concerns, yet measures developed in one cultural context cannot be assumed to function equivalently across populations. The present study evaluated the factor structure and cross- cultural measurement equivalence of the 13-item Appearance-Related Social Media Consciousness (ASMC) Scale among 1,484 university students from Iran (<i>n</i>=974) and the United States (<i>n</i>=510). Confirmatory factor analyses were conducted separately in each sample using diagonally weighted least squares estimation, followed by multigroup tests of measurement invariance. Internal consistency and convergent validity were also examined. The ASMC demonstrated excellent internal consistency in both Iran (<i>α</i>=.954) and the United States (<i>α</i>=.949). Although the initial one-factor model showed strong factor loadings and high CFI and TLI values, absolute fit was inadequate, particularly according to RMSEA. A revised one-factor model incorporating four cross-sample-consistent correlated residuals substantially improved fit, with standardized factor loadings remaining strong across both samples. Multigroup analyses supported configural and threshold invariance, and practical fit indices provided support for approximate metric invariance despite a significant chi-square difference test. In the U.S. sample, ASMC scores were strongly associated with CONAPP scores (<i>r</i>=.85, <i>p</i>=.001), supporting convergent validity. Overall, findings provide qualified support for the ASMC as a predominantly unidimensional and broadly comparable measure across Iranian and U.S. university students, supporting its use in cross-cultural research examining associations involving appearance-related social media consciousness while highlighting the need for further psychometric refinement and replication.",
},
{ "A_ID": 19,
	"title":"The “Cognitive Gym” — Re-examining the RMET in the Wake of COVID-19",
	"authors":"Nilah Lafontant and Wendy Braje",
	"affilation": ["SUNY Plattsburgh, Plattsburgh, NY"],
	"abstract": "Research has found that, in comparison to their neurotypical counterparts, individuals with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) had more difficulty recognizing emotion in faces that were wearing face masks during the coronavirus pandemic (Ventura <i>et al.</i>, 2023, Ramachandran, 2021). We examined whether the common usage of face masks post-pandemic has increased the ability of those with autistic traits to recognize emotion. We measured the relationship between scores on the Baron-Cohen RMET (a test of emotion recognition) and the AQ-10 (a brief assessment of autistic traits). Here we report data collected from 27 students in a psychology lab class at SUNY Plattsburgh. Four participants (15%) scored ≥6 on the AQ-10, indicating possible autistic traits. These 4 participants had an average RMET score of 27.5, while the remaining participants had an average RMET score of 25.8 (<i>t</i><sub>15.36</sub>=1.63, <i>p</i>=.123). While all participants scored within the typical range of the RMET (22–30), there was a non-significant but positive correlation between scores on the two tests (<i>r</i><sub>25</sub>=.377, <i>p</i>=.053). These results indicate that individuals with autistic traits may have improved their ability to read emotions. We plan to collect additional data this fall, which will improve our ability to detect a possible relationship between these two variables.",
},
{   "A_ID": 25,
	"title":"Impacts of peer victimization on social responsiveness in adolescent males",
	"authors":"Katherine M. Hashem, Alexis Brieant, and Anna Beloborodova",
	"affilation": ["University of Vermont, Burlington, VT"],
	"abstract": "While social withdrawal and loneliness are common throughout many presentations of male mental health difficulties, little is known about the trajectories by which low social responsiveness in typically developing males arises. This study seeks to understand whether experiences of victimization predict changes, particularly decreases, in social responsiveness in adolescent boys. This study will run a regression analysis to assess whether victimization scores on the Peer Experience Questionnaire predict scores on the Short Social Responsiveness Scale, using data collected at 3 timepoints by the Adolescent Brain Cognitive Development (ABCD) Study. The ABCD study is longitudinal, following 11,878 participants from 9-10 years old and collecting data on a broad range of developmental domains annually. The present study will focus on ABCD’s male participants, making up approximately 52% of the sample. We predict that social responsiveness will remain moderately stable in the sample overall. Additionally, we predict that higher peer victimization at baseline will be associated with decreases in social responsiveness in adolescent boys at a later timepoint. Results from this study will illuminate whether victimization experiences might contribute to social impairment, a risk factor for male mental health.",
},
{   "A_ID": 22,
	"title":"Perceptions of Personal Purpose: Teleology, Morality, and Identity",
	"authors":"Rae E. Noelting, Chase M. Nichols, and Andrew G. Christy",
	"affilation": ["SUNY Plattsburgh, Plattsburgh, NY"],
	"abstract": "Our study examined students’ views about the world, the self, teleology (belief that there exists a purpose or aim to human lives), and morality. Previous research has found that moral character traits perceived as especially central to who a person is, and people’s “true” selves perceived as morally good by default. Further work has also documented teleological patterns in judgments about the identity of both persons and non-person entities, such that identity persists as long as the entity continues to perform its function/purpose, but identity is disrupted when functional/purposive activity stops. We seek to extend and integrate these lines of research. If people commonly perceive the main “function” or “purpose” of a person to be a moral one, this could help explain why morality is consistently seen as so important to personal identity. We investigated these topics using a self-report survey with correlational and experimental elements. The survey measured general belief in teleology (e.g., “Rain falls in order to allow plants to grow”), teleology about human beings (e.g., “Human beings exist for a purpose”), beliefs about the specific purpose of human lives (one’s own and human life in general), and beliefs about personal identity. Participants also read about characters undergoing changes, and rated the extent to which the character remained the same person post-change. Valence of change (from “bad to good” vs. from “good to bad”) was manipulated between-subjects, and type of change (moral vs. non-moral) was manipulated within-subjects. Data analysis is ongoing, but we predict that those who endorse teleology more strongly will show stronger “moral centering” effects in their judgments about identity, and may believe more strongly in the existence of “true selves.”",
},
{   "A_ID": 14,
	"title":"I-sharing While Depressed",
	"authors":"Natalie A. Fuchs, Elizabeth C. Pinel, Elias Tomaino, Izzy Firneno, and Jenna Sajecki ",
	"affilation": ["University of Vermont, Burlington, VT"],
	"abstract": "Depression often coincides with expectations of rejection and feelings of disconnect. The current study investigates whether, even for people with high depressive symptoms, I-sharing can increase feelings of connectedness. I-sharing refers to moments in which an individual infers that they have had the same subjective experience as another person or persons. I-sharing can foster warmth and liking for the I-share partner and reduce feelings of existential isolation (a construct positively correlated with depression). Participants (<i>N</i>=147) indicated their depression symptoms then underwent an I-sharing manipulation. After, participants reported their perceived positive regard from and perceived connectedness to their partner. Depression symptoms moderated the relationship between I-sharing and participants’ perceived connectedness to their partner. Participants in the I-share condition perceived higher connectedness to their partner as their depression symptoms increased. In the no I-share condition, depression indirectly predicted perceived connectedness (the more depression, the less perceived connectedness) via the mediator, perceived regard. Together, the results suggest that I-sharing experiences may help individuals with depression feel connected to others. ",
},
{   "A_ID": 7,
	"title":"Narrative Comprehension and Inference in Autism Across Visual and Linguistic Modalities",
	"authors":"Katherine E. Westbergᵃ, Olivia Cioccaᵃ, Devon Kearnsᵃ, Holly Chappellᵇ, Caitlyn Soongᵇ, Nicole Sperrazzaᵇ, Emily Zaneᵇ, Neil Cohnᶜ, and Emily Coderreᵃ", 
	"affilation": ["ᵃUniversity of Vermont, Burlington, VT","ᵇJames Madison University, Harrisonburg, VA","ᶜTilburg University, Tilburg, Netherland"],
	"abstract": "Autistic individuals have previously been reported to have differences in narrative comprehension. Studies have shown that narrative comprehension differs in autistic individuals across both visual modalities (pictures or comics) and linguistic modalities (written or spoken language). It has also been shown that inference generation is a vital component of narrative comprehension. However, few studies have investigated this process of inference generation across both visual and linguistic modalities, and fewer have examined this specifically in the autistic population. To explore this further, the present study will examine event-related potentials that are associated with narratives that require inferencing. The neural responses will be compared between inference and non-inference conditions, as well as between both linguistic and visual modalities. Individual differences (such as autistic traits and fluency) will also be analyzed to see how they might modulate these responses. This study aims to provide insight into the extent in which this specific subfunction contributes to differences observed in narrative comprehension in ASD.",
},
{   "A_ID": 10,
	"title":"The Influence of Physical Attractiveness on Health Related Stigma",
	"authors":"John P. DePalma, Shahrzad Ahmadkaraji, Ginger Sage, Willa Crowley, and Antonio Cepeda-Benito",
	"affilation": ["University of Vermont, Burlington, VT"],
	"abstract": "Background: Stigma influences patient treatment plans and outcomes in healthcare. This study measured stigma directly by adapting the halo and horns effects, using photo sets to assess participants' biases. We hypothesized that more attractive faces would be sorted into health-positive behavior categories more often, and health-risk categories less often, than less attractive faces, with the inverse pattern for less attractive faces. Methods: A survey was administered to 298 University of Vermont college students. Participants sorted photo sets of faces into groups based on how likely each pictured person was to perform a paired health-related behavior, categorized as health positive, health neutral, or health risk. This sorting served as the direct measure of stigma. Stimuli comprised six male and six female sets of six photos each, each set represented one of six races/ethnicities and contained two high, two average, and two low attractiveness faces. Results: A 3 (behavior type: positive, neutral, risk) &times, 2 (target gender: men, women) factorial ANOVA was conducted. Each sorting category was scored per participant: high attractiveness faces received 1, average attractiveness faces 0, and low attractiveness faces −1. Higher scores therefore indicated that more attractive faces were sorted into that category more often. Analyses revealed a significant effect of behavior type and a significant interaction between behavior type and target gender. Discussion: The significant ANOVA results supported our hypotheses. More attractive faces were sorted into health-positive categories more often and health-risk categories less often than less attractive faces, and the inverse held for less attractive faces.",
},
{   "A_ID": 21,
	"title":"Extensive Counterconditioning Does Not Reduce Renewal and Proposed Role of the Ventral Hippocampus",
	"authors":"Paige L. Grover, Shaina L. Weingart, Zoey B. Greenbaum, Neil E. Winterbauer, and Travis P. Todd",
	"affilation":[ "University of Vermont, Burlington, VT"],
	"abstract": "Counterconditioning can be used to replace a conditioned response (CR) with a new one. This is done through the pairing of a conditioned stimulus (CS) with a new unconditioned stimulus (US) of the opposite motivational valence (e.g., food pellet) than the original US (e.g., footshock). For example, with rodents, after initial tone-shock pairings, the same tone might then be paired with sucrose pellets. However, counterconditioned responses are highly context-dependent and the original CR is vulnerable to relapse. For instance, freezing that was reduced during counterconditioning can renew when the CS is presented outside the counterconditioning context. Increasing the number of extinction trials (overtraining) attenuates renewal in some studies but not others, and to our knowledge it has not been tested after counterconditioning. We therefore tested whether extended counterconditioning weakens ABA renewal. To do so, rats received fear conditioning followed by either 60 or 180 counterconditioning trials, then tested in the original conditioning context. Renewal, measured by freezing at the CS presentation, was observed irrespective of the number of counterconditioning trials. To examine its neural basis, we propose to test the role of the ventral hippocampus, which is thought to gate competing aversive and appetitive associations through connections with the prelimbic and infralimbic cortices. We will examine how this mechanism is impacted through chemogenetic inactivation of the ventral hippocampus during counterconditioning to test whether this prevents the context-specific learning of counterconditioning and reduces renewal. ",
},
{   "A_ID": 20,
	"title":"Pain Intensity, Emotion Regulation, and Substance Use Willingness in Emerging Adults With Chronic Pain",
	"authors":"Olivia R. Hansen",
	"affilation": ["University of Vermont, Burlington, VT"],
	"abstract": "<i>Background:</i> Chronic pain is associated with increased risk for substance use, particularly during emerging adulthood, a developmental period characterized by developing decision-making skills and heightened peer influence. Identifying factors associated with substance use willingness, the openness to using substances when the opportunity arises, may help clarify substance use risk prior to problematic patterns developing. This study examines the associations between pain intensity, emotion regulation difficulties, and substance use willingness among emerging adults with chronic pain. <p><i>Methods:</i> Participants were 225 emerging adults with chronic pain who completed an online cross-sectional survey. Pain intensity was assessed using the PROMIS Pain Intensity - Short Form. Emotion regulation difficulties were assessed using the strategies and goals subscales of the Difficulties in Emotion Regulation Scale - Short Form. Substance use willingness was assessed utilizing an adapted form of Gerrad <i>et al.</i> (2008)’s prototype willingness measure which measures willingness across hypothetical social scenarios involving alcohol, cannabis, cigarettes, and non-prescribed illicit drugs. <p><i>Hypotheses:</i> Greater pain intensity is hypothesized to be associated with greater substance use willingness. Greater emotion regulation difficulties, including limited access to effective regulation strategies and difficulties maintaining goal-directed behavior during negative emotional states, are expected to be associated with greater substance use willingness. Finally, pain intensity and emotion regulation difficulties are expected to explain overlapping variance in substance use willingness. <p><i>Results/Conclusions:</i> Analysis of this dataset is ongoing. Examining these associations may improve understanding of substance use risk factors among emerging adults with chronic pain and inform future prevention and early intervention efforts.",
},


{   "A_ID": 9,
	"title":"Environmental Enrichment and L-Theanine Supplementation on Behavioral Responses in a Preclinical Model of Anxiety-like Behavior: A Pilot Study",
	"authors":"Nela Chestojanova and Adam E. Fox",
	"affilation": "St. Lawrence University",
	"abstract": "We examined whether environmental enrichment or L-theanine supplementation alters anxiety-like and exploratory behavior in male Long-Evans rats following acute restraint stress. Given the small sample (<i>N</i>=12) and emphasis on individual behavioral change, this study served as an exploratory pilot evaluation.  Following a 5-minute forced restraint stress intervention, 16-week-old male Long-Evans rats were randomly assigned to control (<i>n</i>=4), environmental enrichment (<i>n</i>=4), or L-theanine supplementation (<i>n</i>=4) groups. Animals completed two pretest and two posttest elevated plus maze (EPM) sessions. Individual change scores (posttest minus pretest) were calculated for primary anxiety outcomes (percentage of open-arm time and entries) and locomotor activity (distance traveled), and evaluated using one-way ANOVAs. No significant group differences were observed for primary anxiety-related measures. Mean change in percentage of open-arm time was +5.59% in controls, −0.10% in enriched rats, and +10.63% in L-theanine rats, <i>F</i><sub>2,9</sub>=0.86, <i>p</i>=.456. Exploratory analysis of distance traveled revealed the largest reduction in enriched rats (−6.41 m), compared with −1.69 m in controls and +0.91 m in L-theanine rats. L-theanine showed the largest numerical increase in open-arm time, whereas enrichment was characterized by a consistent reduction in distance traveled. These preliminary findings suggest that environmental enrichment and L-theanine may influence behavioral adaptation to repeated EPM exposure and require verification in larger, adequately powered studies",
},
{   "A_ID": 8,
	"title":"Narrative Episodic Future Thinking's Effect on Discounting in a Group of Risk-Averse Participants",
	"authors":"Seth Gustafson and A. E. Fox",
	"affilation": "St. Lawrence University",
	"abstract": "Delay discounting is the decline in the present value of reward with delay to its receipt. Excessive delay discounting has been referred to as a trans-disease process underlying choice behavior. However, “risk-aversive” individuals may present opposite delay discounting behavior and seem “less impulsive” in delay discounting tasks and probability discounting tasks. Excessive risk aversion is associated with maladaptive cognitive emotion regulation strategies such as rumination and difficulty updating working memory related to new information. Narrative episodic future thinking (NEFT) is a psychological intervention involving vividly imagining specific thematically connected future events that has been seen to alter delay discounting and decision making. The current study recruited 1 participant with the goal of eventually recruiting 5 to 10 risk-averse participants, determined by their scores on a baseline delay discounting and probability discounting task, to engage in narrative episodic future thinking prompts. The participant took a measurement of delay discounting and probability discounting once a week across 8 weeks and engaged with NEFT 4 out of the 8 weeks. According to our hypotheses, NEFT should induce a change in discounting so that participants become less “risk-averse” and more comfortable with positive risky situations. In other words, they should have higher rates of delay discounting in the NEFT phase. ",
},
{   "A_ID": 29,
	"title":"Investigating the Joint Effect of Autism Spectrum Disorder and Early-Life Adversity on Youth Mental Health Outcomes",
	"authors":"Emma Renwick and Alexis Brieant",
	"affilation": "University of Vermont",
	"abstract": "Prior research indicates that 58% of children 18 years or younger have experienced at least one form of early-life adversity (ELA) (Madigan <i>et al.</i>, 2025). Youth with diagnoses of autism spectrum disorder (ASD), herein referred to as autistic youth, are at increased risk of experiencing childhood adversity compared to their non-autistic peers (Kerns <i>et al.</i>, 2017). Higher reports of ELA increases the likelihood of poor mental health outcomes amongst both autistic and non-autistic youth (Wiggins <i>et al.</i>, 2025). However, there is minimal research investigating the joint effect of ELA and ASD on youth mental health. Thus, we propose the following questions: 1) does a diagnosis of ASD increase the likelihood of internalizing symptoms (e.g., anxiety, depression) in youth, 2) does ELA increase the likelihood of later internalizing symptoms, and 3) does the interaction between ELA and ASD increase the likelihood of later internalizing symptoms? We hypothesize that the interaction of ASD and ELA will increase the internalizing symptoms later in adolescence such that youth with ASD who have experienced higher levels of adversity will demonstrate the greatest internalizing symptoms. Data will be drawn from the Adolescent Brain Cognitive Development (ABCD) Study, a longitudinal study of youth development in the United States (<i>n</i>=11,878). ASD will be treated as a grouping variable to signify whether a diagnosis was reported at any time point. We will use a composite measure of overall ELA exposure at baseline (9-10 years old). Internalizing symptoms will be indexed with the Child Behavior Checklist (CBCL). We will test a multiple regression model to examine main and interaction effects. The findings of this study will inform future research on the role of ELA in autistic youth mental health.",
},
{   "A_ID": 11,
	"title":"Image Exposure and Psychological Reactivity in College Students",
	"authors":"Willa Crowley, Ginger Sage,  John DePalma,  Shahrzad Ahmadkaraji, and  Antonio Cepeda-Benito",
	"affilation": "University of Vermont",
	"abstract": "Past research has linked social comparison with body image dissatisfaction, particularly among young women. This study attempts to replicate the work of Moreno-Padilla <i>et al.</i> (2025) using a between-subjects design and will also examine gender identity and sexual orientation as potential moderating variables. College students will be randomly assigned to view images of swimsuit models with idealized bodies, plus-sized swimsuit models, or home furnishings (control). Participants in the experimental conditions will be prompted to visualize themselves in the modeled swimsuit and answer six associated questions. Self-comparison tendencies and body appreciation will be assessed at the end of the survey. Additionally, participants will be exposed to four sets of food images: fruits, vegetables, ultra-processed sweet, and ultra-processed savory. It is hypothesized that (1) participants exposed to images of idealized bodies will report less favorable body-related evaluations than those exposed to non-idealized or neutral images, (2) social comparison will moderate body-related evaluations, with individuals higher in comparison orientation showing stronger condition effects, (3) individuals reporting more same-gender attraction will show attenuated responses to idealized images, (4) female-identifying individuals will show stronger condition effects than male-identifying individuals, 5) participants who view images of idealized bodies will show greater cravings for ultra-processed foods than those who view images of non-idealized bodies or neutral images.",
},

{   "A_ID": 23,
	"title":"Memories of the 2024 solar eclipse: Clear or cloudy?",
	"authors":"Steve Mansfield, Wendy Braje,  Dayton Cubillo-Dreher,  Dana Delles,  Kathryn Guyer,  Christine Parmeter,  Hannah Peroza, and  Grace Racicot",
	"affilation": "SUNY Plattsburgh",
	"abstract": "The SUNY Plattsburgh campus was in the direct path of the April 2024 solar eclipse, offering a unique opportunity to study autobiographical memories. We compared immediate reports of details of the eclipse with memories of those details 11 months later. Here we report findings for memories of the weather, which we found to be only 50% accurate overall. This is surprising given that participants were typically outside looking at the sky for over an hour.",
},
{   "A_ID": 28,
	"title":"Racial Queer Microaffirmation Project (RQMaP)",
	"authors":"Abby R. Wannamaker and Lamont Stanley Bryant",
	"affilation": "University of Vermont",
	"abstract": "Black lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender, and queer (LGBTQ+) young adults develop their identities in an increasingly anti-Black and anti-queer climate. Microaffirmations, or affirming identity-related interpersonal messages, can buffer the negative effects of stigma and discrimination and promote well-being among queer and trans people of color. Yet researchers know little about how distinct types of microaffirmations, and who delivers them, shape Black queer identity. This gap matters because the Racial Queer Identity framework describes racial and queer identities as developing together amid racism and heterosexism, affirmation that addresses only one identity may miss how Black queer young adults experience support. We propose semi-structured interviews with a national sample of Black LGBTQ+ young adults (ages 18–24). Guided by this framework, the study aims to (1) explore how four types of microaffirmations (microrecognitions, microvalidations, microtransformations, and microprotections) inform participants' relationship to their Black queer identity, (2) understand how sharing, or not sharing, identities with the person offering a microaffirmation shapes its meaning for participants, and (3) explore how participants weigh the relative impact of each type. Participants will describe experiences with each type, then rank the types in a card-sort activity and explain their reasoning. Using reflexive thematic analysis, we will generate themes from interview transcripts, including participants' explanations of their rankings. This study will offer insight into how Black LGBTQ+ young adults make meaning of affirmation, extending the Racial Queer Identity framework and informing how families, peers, educators, and providers support Black queer identity development.",
},
{   "A_ID": 24,
	"title":"Political Ideology and Positive Sexual Socialization",
	"authors":"Kayla Joyce, Lily Dubin, and  Theo Constantinescu",
	"affilation": "University of Vermont",
	"abstract": "According to the CDC, 28.5% of high school students engaged in sexual intercourse in 2025, and less than 50% of sexually active adolescents used a condom the last time they engaged in sexual intercourse. Therefore, it is critical to understand how children are learning about sex and sexuality to promote sexual health. Many adolescents consider parents to be a primary source of sexual education and socialization (Albert, 2010). Many factors can influence how parents sexually socialize their children, especially political ideology (Jeannet <i>et al.</i>, 2023). For example, conservative ideologies value more traditional or religious views of sex and sexuality while more liberal views highlight acceptance and medically-accurate education. The goal of this analysis is to examine the association between parents' political ideology and their levels of positive sexual socialization. This study was conducted on Prolific.com in July 2023. Participants are a binary gender-balanced sample of 499 parents (49.9% White, 50.1% POC). Parents answered a series of open-ended questions asking (1) if they incorporated sex and sexuality into their parenting, (2) how they did so, or (3) why they did not. Members of the Family Development Laboratory collaborated in pairs and qualitatively coded the parent's responses to the open-ended questions through multiple rounds. We hypothesize that there is a positive relationship between liberal political ideology and positive sexual socialization, and a negative relationship with socialization messages citing religion and/or traditional values. We will test this hypothesis using multiple regressions. Understanding these correlations can inform future sexual socialization interventions like school-based educational policies to ensure each child has access to the sexual health information they need. ",
},
{   "A_ID": 3,
	"title":"Racial Differences in Color Evasion in Parent Racial Socialization Messages",
	"authors":"Jamie L. Abaied, Issac Lubin,  Paige MacGibbon,  Amani Patel, and  Maggie Ricciardiello",
	"affilation": "University of Vermont",
	"abstract": "Color-blind racial ideology is defined as either the denial or downplaying of systemic racism (power evasion) and/or the promotion of the idea that race doesn’t matter and that we are all the same (Neville <i>et al.</i>, 2013). The goal of this analysis is to compare instances of color evasion in parent responses to the prompt, “What do you want your child to know/understand about race in America?” across racial groups. The study was conducted on Prolific.com in July 2023. We recruited a gender-balanced and racially diverse sample (241 men, 243 women, 50% White, 25.9% Black, 12.1% Latinx, 6.1% Asian, 4.1% Multiracial, 1% Native/Indigenous, <i>M</i> age = 39.85, <i>SD</i> = 7.13) of U.S.-resident parents of youth aged 8-17 years (247 boys, 238 girls, 47.4% White, 23.2% Black, 12.7% Multiracial, 10.3% Latinx, 4.7% Asian, 0.6% Native/Indigenous, <i>M</i> age = 12.53, <i>SD</i> = 2.81). Parents answered a series of open-ended questions asking (1) if they incorporated race into their parenting, (2) how they did so, or (3) why they did not. Members of the Family Development Laboratory collaborated in pairs and qualitatively coded the parent's responses to the open-ended questions through multiple rounds. In our planned analyses, we will explore the overlap and divergence between different racial groups of parents in the ways they express color evasion. We will compare the frequency of color evasion between racial groups and use direct quotes from parent responses to provide more context for racial differences in color evasion.",
},
{   "A_ID": 26,
	"title":"Parental Attitudes Toward Gender Diversity",
	"authors":"Nathan J. Klonel, Laura MacMullin, and  Samantha Stephens",
	"affilation": "Middlebury College",
	"abstract": "National conversations about government and education often focus on parents’ roles in deciding what children learn about gender identity, expression, and stereotypes. Parental views frame how children's exposure to topics on gender diversity are both discussed and implemented or regulated. Yet, little is known about the content of and experiences informing these views. To better understand caregivers’ attitudes toward gender nonconformity and gender diversity in their own children, we will analyse survey responses from parents of 4- to 7-year-old children. Respondents (<i>n</i>=89) were recruited during the summer of 2026 through paid social media advertising and community outreach and were primarily from the New England area. The survey was administered virtually through Zoom, as part of a larger parent study. Respondents filled out four open text-response questions intended to gauge parents’ comfort with and reactions to their own child’s exposure to topics of gender diversity (e.g., “How do you feel about your child learning about topics such as gender stereotypes, gender identity, and/or gender expression? Why?”). This semester, we will use content analysis to discover themes and interrogate nuances in caregivers’ attitudes and experiences regarding gender nonconformity. Our poster will report initial thematic findings and broad observations across topics and respondents. This work can inform implementation of gender diversity education in elementary curriculum as well as interventions aimed at improving attitudes toward gender diversity. Continued research should further investigate factors that predict caregiver attitudes towards topics of gender diversity and the relationship between caregivers’ and children's attitudes.",
},
{   "A_ID": 1,
	"title":"Communication Competence Among College Students",
	"authors":"Sebrin Aragaw",
	"affilation": "SUNY Plattsburgh",
	"abstract": "Family is one of the first places where people learn how to communicate with others. Some people grow up in families where they are encouraged to openly share their opinions, talk about their feelings, disagree, and be part of conversations, while others may have fewer opportunities to do so. These early differences in communication may be related to how people communicate with others later in life. The present study will examine the relationship between family conversation orientation and interpersonal communication competence among college students. It is hypothesized that students who report more open family communication will also report higher interpersonal communication competence. College students aged 18 years or older will complete two self-report measures. Family communication will be assessed using the Revised Family Communication Patterns Instrument, with conversation orientation as the main variable of interest. Interpersonal communication competence will be assessed using the Interpersonal Communication Competence Scale, which measures several areas of communication, including self-disclosure, empathy, social relaxation, assertiveness, interaction management, and supportiveness. A correlational analysis will be used to examine the relationship between the two variables. Because the study is correlational, the findings cannot determine whether family communication causes differences in later communication competence. However, the findings may help us better understand whether the way people learn to communicate within their families is still reflected in how they connect and communicate with others as young adults.",
},
{   "A_ID": 18,
	"title":"Does Therapy Work? Exploring Students' Mental-Health Beliefs and Attitudes",
	"authors":"Dayvion D. L. Cruz, Meah Buntz, and  Andrew G. Christy",
	"affilation": "SUNY Plattsburgh",
	"abstract": "We are designing an online survey-based study of students’ beliefs and attitudes surrounding mental health, and factors that may be associated with those beliefs and attitudes. In particular, we plan to examine beliefs about the causes of mental disorders, perceptions of how severe and debilitating they are, and the degree to which different kinds of treatment (e.g., biomedical vs. psychosocial treatments) are seen as appropriate. We will explore these beliefs and attitudes both towards mental disorders in general, and towards specific classes of disorders that especially interest us (addiction, suicidality, neurodevelopmental disorders, and eating disorders). We also plan to examine various factors that we expect to be related to mental-health beliefs and attitudes, including students’ own self-reported mental health, parenting and attachment styles, degree of personal and social exposure to mental disorders and treatments, and demographic factors including gender, socioeconomic status, political and religious orientations, and race/ethnicity. We expect that students with a more extensive history of direct or social exposure to mental health issues will have less negative attitudes towards mental disorders overall (i.e., score lower on stigma) and more favorable attitudes towards mainstream forms of biomedical and psychosocial treatment. Insecure attachment (particularly avoidance) is expected to predict more negative attitudes towards treatment-seeking. Beyond testing these hypotheses, we will also explore how other factors are associated with students’ mental-health beliefs and attitudes.",
},
{   "A_ID": 27, 
	"title":"Parental Relationships and College Student Mental Health and Social Relationships",
	"authors":"Shana A. Wright and Preeti Samudra",
	"affilation": "SUNY Plattsburgh",
	"abstract": "The ways parents raise their children are the very foundation of how we develop. The purpose of the present study is to see how college students recollect the parenting they received when they were younger, as well as the correlation between these childhood parenting styles and their interpersonal relationship quality and mental health. In the proposed study, I plan to recruit 100 college student participants to complete an online questionnaire that measures: recollections of childhood relationships with parents, current mental health, current romantic relationships, and current platonic relationships. The measures that we will be using are the Parental Bonding Instrument (PBI), DASS-21, Friendship Quality Questionnaire (FQQ), and Adult Attachment Scale (AAS), and Relationship Assessment Scale (RAS). Some of the parenting scales will be adapted to assess recollections of past parenting as required. We expect to find that more supportive/secure/authoritative recollections of relationships with their parents during childhood will correlate with higher interpersonal relationship quality and lower mental health difficulty scores. Understanding the connection our childhood familial relationships could have with our adult selves is very important to understanding potential sources of our strengths and struggles as well as possible avenues to focus on to improve our future health and wellbeing.",
},


{   "A_ID": 5,
	"title":"Does Mechanism Matter? Neuroscience Message Framing and Attitudes Toward Psychedelic-Assisted Therapy",
	"authors":"Matthew M. Armstrongᵃ, Jennifer Bremserᵃ,  and Timothy Zagarᵇ",
	"affilation": ["ᵃSUNY Plattsburgh","ᵇGlens Falls Hospital, NY"],
	"abstract": "Over the past 2 decades, research on psychedelic-assisted therapies has rapidly expanded, with clinical evidence suggesting potential benefits for treating conditions ranging from post-traumatic stress disorder and treatment resistant depression to cluster headaches and phantom limb pain. However, unresolved policy questions remain regarding treatment labeling, safe administration, provider roles, and training requirements. In April 2026, an executive order directed federal agencies to accelerate psychedelic research, drug evaluation and pathways to access. To comply with this order, the FDA convened a public hearing on September 14th inviting researchers, clinicians, industry representatives, patient advocates, and people with lived experience to share their perspectives.  As psychedelic-assisted therapies move toward clinical implementation, how they are described to patients and providers may influence whether they are viewed as safe, credible, and effective. Many classic psychedelics remain Schedule 1 controlled substances and are associated with historical and social stigma. Meanwhile, research has advanced understanding of their neurobiological mechanisms, including their potential to induce a temporary period of enhanced neuroplasticity. Neuroscience based explanations may offer one way to communicate how these treatments work, potentially reducing stigma. The present study tests whether describing the neurobiological mechanisms of psychedelic assisted psychotherapy influences treatment attitudes. We hypothesize that participants who receive a neuroscience-framed description will report less stigma, greater perceived safety, and stronger intentions to consider treatment than participants who receive a psychological description. Findings will be interpreted through the lens of neuroscience message framing, which suggests biological explanations may carry greater weight because they provide a concrete account of complex psychological phenomena. https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/epub/10.1177/25158163251345472   https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29764303/",
},

{   "A_ID": 15,
	"title":"Interactions Over Social Media",
	"authors":"Isabel Firneno, Natalie Fuchs,  Jenna Sajecki,  Asher Winsten-Pinel, and Elizabeth Pinel.",
	"affilation": "University of Vermont",
	"abstract": "Using social media to communicate with others undoubtedly has implications for the interpersonal behavior enacted, and the relationships that form, through this medium. Social media usage can strengthen preexisting relationships and help users maintain long-distance relationships. However, using SnapChat, compared to FaceBook, encourages problematic behaviors (e.g., sending/receiving nude photos) and less relationship confidence (e.g., more romantic jealousy). These findings imply that specific app features have implications for user safety and well-being via their influence on user behaviors and relationship-relevant variables.  In the proposed study, we examine three app features that likely affect online behavior.  Participants will imagine that they begin communicating, via a new app called “Wave,” with a romantic interest they previously met at a party. We will manipulate three features of Wave: permanence of messages (permanent or disappearing), visuals in the app (some visuals or no visuals), and timing of responses with the romantic interest (simultaneous responses or noticeable delay). We will measure the effect of our manipulations on feelings of closeness, risqué behavior, and participant willingness to tolerate questionable behaviors from a romantic prospect. We expect to find the highest feelings of closeness, and the most risqué behaviors and tolerance of questionable behaviors when Wave features include impermanent messages, visuals, and simultaneous responses. Generally, we hope to get greater insight into which app features most affect user behaviors, and thus to better understand how modern relationships unfold via social media communication.",
},
{   "A_ID": 6,
	"title":"Double Trouble: The Learning of Multiple Mappings in the Context of Cross Situational Word Learning",
	"authors":"Felicity F. Frinsel, Emily McArdle, and  Faline H. Yang ",
	"affilation": "SUNY Plattsburgh",
	"abstract": "A key task for language learners is to determine which referents reliably cooccur with specific words. Numerous studies have shown that adults and infants can learn word–referent mappings (1:1) from cross-situational statistics. However, natural language learning often involves greater complexity, such as homonyms, where one word maps to multiple referents (1:2), and synonyms, where multiple words map to the same referent (2:1). We investigated how learning differs across these three conditions. Participants were randomly assigned to one of the three groups and learned word–referent mappings over several training blocks followed by a test phase. Results showed that overall performance was above chance in all groups. However, one-to-one form–meaning mappings were learned significantly more efficiently than mappings involving any type of ambiguity. Moreover, the specific direction of ambiguity mattered less than the mere presence of competition. That is, in the absence of feedback, only the 1:1 condition showed continued improvement across blocks, whereas performance in the 2:1 and 1:2 conditions plateaued and did not differ from one another. The continued difficulty with homonym and synonym mappings points to more than just slower learning, the plateau suggests that learners may require additional cues to resolve competition.",
},

{   "A_ID": 17,
	"title":"Developmental & Differential Appraisals of Violation of Expectation Events from 6 to 8 months",
	"authors":"Mallorie Putvain and Katalina Corp",
	"affilation": "Vermont State University", 
	"abstract": "The violation of expectation (VOE) paradigm is routinely used to discover infants’ reactions to events that violate natural laws. Most VOE studies have been performed in asocial settings, which are devoid of important social information that infants rely on to make sense of experiences, while recent VOE studies performed in social settings have shown to influence infant reactions to VOE events. Using a 2&times,3 mixed factorial design, this study aims to compare the reactions of 6-month-olds (<i>N</i>=45) verses 8-month-olds (<i>N</i>=45), across two different VOE types (impossible/magic & improbable/humorous), as well as a control event. Each event is preceded by a bell to draw the infant’s attention, and each event is repeated four times by the researcher in clear view reciting a script. Each condition is performed with two distinct objects, a red ball and red straw, for a total of six events. Events are video recorded and will be coded by two researchers who are blind to the hypotheses. We predict an effect for event type, with infants looking/reaching more toward impossible/magic VOEs, verses smiling/laughing more at improbable/humorous VOEs regardless of age. We also predict an effect for ages, with 6 and 8-month-olds showing different degrees of these responses and an interaction effect showing developmental differences in appraisals of impossible/magical versus improbable/humorous VOEs.",
},
{   "A_ID": 2,
	"title":"Agentic Willpower and its Relation to Burnout, Procrastination, and GPA",
	"authors":"Alexa Leavine, Evan Kaye,  Edward Sturman, Shana Santos and Data Murdie",
	"affilation": "SUNY Plattsburgh",
	"abstract": "In the present study, we will be evaluating whether a newer personality construct, termed agentic willpower, is related to changes in burnout, procrastination, and GPA in undergraduate students. Willpower has traditionally been studied in Psychology from the perspective of self-control but agentic willpower captures the striving aspect of willpower, incorporating grit, resilience, self-efficacy, and an internal locus of control.  We hypothesized that higher levels of agentic willpower would predict less burnout, procrastination, and, ultimately, higher GPA over the course of a semester. Undergraduate Psychology students (ideally 100-200) will be recruited from a variety of course levels (i.e. 1st year Introduction to Psychology to 4th year Psychology electives) and awarded course credit for their participation. Based on the demographics of the student body, participants will likely be predominantly Caucasian and female. They will be assessed at two time points: once in early October and a second point in late November/early December. Measures at both time points will include the following: Demographic information, Agentic Willpower Scale, Burnout Assessment Tool for Students, and General Procrastination Scale. GPA records will be collected at the end of the semester. A path analysis will be used to evaluate whether latent change scores in burnout and procrastination mediate the relationship between baseline agentic willpower scores and GPA at the end of the semester.",
},
{   "A_ID": 13,
	"title":"Campers turned counselors: The experience of transition in a medical and disability specialty camp",
	"authors":"Alyvia M. Gellᵃ, Jonathan Gerthᵇ, and  Jennifer Bremserᵃ",
	"affilation": ["ᵃSUNY Plattsburgh","ᵇDouble H Ranch"],
	"abstract": "The goal of this study was to gain insight into seasonal staff experiences of transition from camper to counselor at a medical and disability specialty camp (MDSC). More specifically, we asked the following: 1) how prior experience as a camper affects one’s experience as a counselor at camp, 2) how the transition from camper to counselor may shift one’s perception of self and their place within their community of practice, and 3) to capture the thoughts and feelings associated with the transitional period from camper to counselor.  This qualitative study was conducted at a residential MDSC during a 50-day period. 21 semi-structured interviews were conducted during training sessions and inter sessions during Summer 2026 at Double H Ranch. The main themes found included perception of self, camper experience influencing counselor practices, former camper syndrome, and old camp. Subthemes included gaining practical counselor skills, capability to be a counselor, emphasizing counselor and camper relationships, having role models, a change in their relationship with camp, and the influences of COVID. ",
},

{   "A_ID": 16,
	"title":"The effect of gum flavor, music genre, and visual stimuli on mood.",
	"authors":"Jeannette L. Schultz-Aboujaoude, Kelly Mahoney, and  Benjamin Isabelle", 
	"affilation": "Vermont State University",
	"abstract": "Research has shown that the amygdala remains consistent when viewing neutral stimuli, but negative mood increased, and positive feelings decreased when consuming negative stimuli. Chewing gum has also been found to impact mood. Both spearmint and nonflavored gums have been found to have a positive effect on mood measured through alertness and hedonic tone. As for background music, classical music has been shown to boost mood and self-acceptance. However, the effects of listening to genres like rock or metal are more complicated. Some studies claim that it also has a positive effect on mood while others claim that it has a detrimental effect and correlates with aggressive and anxious tendencies. We had 19 student participants for this study, 15 of which were female, with a mean age of 21. Participants were randomly split into four groups. Each of the groups went through each level of all variables (watermelon or spearmint gum, negative or neutral visual stimuli, metal or classical background music). Each participant went through two trials of the study with opposing conditions. For each trial, a projector played one of two slideshows consisting of 12 neutral images, and 12 distractor images which acted as our visual stimuli. To distinguish between the two types of images, the neutral photos were of simple objects while the stimuli photos were of faces. Participants chewed gum and listened to music before, during, and after the slideshow, and while filling out the mood questionnaire (PANAS). The results of this study did not support previous findings and it had no significant findings itself.",
},


 ];